ctangle like length times  width so that's where that formula comes  from so lateral area equals premier the  base times the height perimeter we can  see is going to be 16 and then the  height is the distance between the two  bases the two squares which is 10 that  gives us a lateral area of 160  centimeters squared so with area you  want to have units squared volume it's  going to be units cubed length is just  the units  but now to find the surface area we want  to take the lateral area which is the  area of the sides but we also want to  add on the two bases the top and the  bottom now the bottom you can see it's a  square four times four is 16 times two  since we have two of them right so  that's gonna be 32 so if we just add on  another 32 centimeters squared our  surface area sometimes referred to as  the total area is gonna be a hundred and  92 centimeters squared and then the last  one is the volume and that's going to be  the area of the base C capital B area of  the base which in this case you can see  four times four it's a square that's 16  times the height which is 10 it's kind  of like taking a stack of sticky notes  you've got a square and you keep  stacking them up that gives you the  third dimension or the volume and so  here for volume we have area the base 16  times the height 10 which is a hundred  and 60 but because it's volume it's  centimeters cubed it's like you're  filling this up with like little ice  cubes and that's how many would contain  be contained inside here now let's go to  number two a little bit more challenging  here see if you can do this one we want  to find the lateral area surface area  and volume of this triangular prism see  how the top and bottom roots are  triangles this was a square prism this  is a hexagonal prism okay so first thing  I want to do is want to find the  perimeter of the base times the height  so the perimeter the base is going to be  the distance all the way around this  triangle three four oh they didn't tell  us what that site is well because it's a  right triangle we can do the Pythagorean  theorem to find this hypotenuse the side  across from the right angle that's our C  side so we have C squared equals a  squared plus B squared C squared equals  3 squared plus 4 squared 3 squared is 9  4 squared of 16 C squared equals 25 and  if we take the square root we get 5 okay  so now we can find the perimeter it's  just going to be 3 plus 4 is 7 plus 5 is  12 so that's 12 times the height which  is the distance between the two  triangles that's 8 and that's going to  give us 96  inches squared for a lateral area now  how about for the surface area well  we're going to take the lateral area  which is the area of the sides plus the  area of the base times two since we have  a top and a bottom the only thing is is  that when you find the area of a  triangle we have to use the triangle  area formula which is one-half base  times height so let's see so 1/2 times  the base which would be three times the  height which is four remember the height  is perpendicular to the base that's  going to give us six but remember we  have two of those we have a top and a  bottom so that's 2 times 6 which is 12  then we just have to add the 12 to the  area of the size which is 96 and that's  going to give us a hundred and eight  inches squared that's the total area or  the surface area now if you want to find  the volume you want to find the area of  the base okay times the height and  that's gonna be your volume but the area  of the base we already figured out that  that was six okay for the triangle times  the height eight so that's just going to  be six times eight which is 48 inches  cubed and that's the total volume now  let's look at number three this one's a  little bit more challenging okay for  number three you might be saying Mario  can I just find the area of like each  face and then add them all up to get the  total surface area you can definitely do  that and a lot of students 